  "summary": "Seven fMRI and EEG preprints link cognitive state, trait dynamics and sensory precision to distributed, low-dimensional brain organization rather than fixed modules.",
  "synthesis": "The preprints treat brain organization as distributed and state-dependent rather than modular. Structure-function coupling varies with cognitive state, strongest during movie-watching and weakest under working memory load. Resting-state fMRI from 994 HCP participants links spatial and temporal network organization to trait-like dynamics. A meta-analysis of 912 papers finds no single core for self-related processing, describing shifting coalitions of systems. Oddball detection follows a shared low-dimensional state landscape with modality-dependent later trajectories. Information decomposition of film-viewing fMRI maps storage, transfer and transformation, and a signed dynamic connectivity framework relates subcortical routing to network flexibility and behavioral traits. EEG work ties sensory representational precision to alpha power, pupil dilation and correlated noise. New here is the emphasis on dynamic, low-dimensional state descriptions, often from naturalistic paradigms.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
